            "The idea of doing nothing arises from a misunderstanding within Buddhism itself.",
            "The first misunderstanding comes from recognizing that the precepts prohibit wrongdoing, then stopping there and forgetting the other half: actively doing good. Practitioners imagine that avoiding wrongdoing is sufficient for the practice leading to liberation. It is not. Avoiding wrongdoing must go hand in hand with actively doing good, as the Buddha taught in the Dhammapada.",
            "The second misunderstanding concerns the Prajñāpāramitā teachings of the Mahāyāna scriptures. These sutras, including the Diamond Sutra, teach Bodhisattva practitioners to perform countless meritorious deeds without clinging to them or taking credit for them. Yet many people mistake this for an instruction to do nothing."
